 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Coil current",
   "expr": "ICOIL = (VSUP – VCE(SAT)) / RCOIL",
   "where": {
    "VSUP": "coil supply voltage",
    "VCE(SAT)": "low-level output voltage of the channel (VOL), from the saturation curves",
    "RCOIL": "coil resistance"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 14
  },
  {
   "name": "On-chip power dissipation",
   "expr": "PD = Σ (i = 1 to N) VOLi × ILi",
   "where": {
    "N": "number of channels on at the same time",
    "VOLi": "voltage at output i while it sinks ILi, equal to VCE(SAT)",
    "ILi": "load current of output i"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 14
  },
  {
   "name": "Maximum allowable dissipation",
   "expr": "PD(MAX) = (TJ(max) – TA) / RθJA",
   "where": {
    "TJ(max)": "target maximum junction temperature",
    "TA": "ambient temperature in operation",
    "RθJA": "junction-to-ambient thermal resistance of the package"
   },
   "note": "PD must not exceed PD(MAX); keep the junction below 125 °C.",
   "page": 14
  },
  {
   "name": "Forward current gain",
   "expr": "hFE = IC / II",
   "where": {
    "IC": "collector current",
    "II": "input current"
   },
   "note": "II is held fixed to measure VCE(sat) and swept to measure hFE.",
   "page": 9
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Channel structure",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Each of the seven channels is an inverting NPN Darlington: the input pin feeds the base of the first transistor through RB (2.7 kΩ in the ULQ2003A, 10.5 kΩ in the ULQ2004A). A 7.2 kΩ resistor across the base-emitter junction of the first transistor and a 3 kΩ resistor across that of the second act as pull-downs and limit leakage from the input. The output transistor's collector is the channel output; a diode from it to COM clamps inductive kick, and a second diode from E up to the collector is a parasitic substrate junction. A further diode runs from E to the input. Resistor values are nominal."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Gain",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "The Darlington pair acts like one transistor with a current gain of about β², up to 10,000 A/A at some currents, so a small input current drives a large output current."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Parasitic diodes",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "The collector-to-emitter diode is a parasitic substrate structure and must not carry current. If a collector can swing below ground, add an external Schottky diode to clamp the undershoot. In normal use the substrate diodes at the inputs and outputs stay reverse biased; forward biasing one turns on parasitic NPN transistors that pull nearly the same current from neighbouring pins."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inductive loads",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "With COM tied to the coil supply, the internal diodes catch the coil current when a channel turns off and return it to the supply, limiting the voltage spike at the output."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Resistive loads",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "A resistive load needs a pull-up to give the output a high level; COM may be left open in that case."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply and COM",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "The part has no supply pin. When COM goes to the system supply, do not let any output rise well above COM: that forward biases the clamp diodes hard, drives a large current into COM, and can damage the on-chip metal or overheat the die."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Paralleling outputs",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Outputs may be connected in parallel when a load needs more current than one channel carries. The saturation voltage of two paralleled channels against their total current is given as a separate curve."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Design procedure",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "For a coil driver, settle four things first: the span of input voltage, the ambient span, the load and base currents, and the heat to shed. The coil current follows from the supply, coil resistance and VCE(sat). The number of coils that can be on together follows from the maximum collector current against duty cycle curve, or more closely from the sum of VOL × IL over all channels kept below PD(MAX)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature grades",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "ULQ200xAT parts run from -40 °C to 105 °C; ULQ2003AQ parts from -40 °C to 125 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "Input traces carry little current and may be thin; keep the input channels apart to limit crosstalk. Make output traces thick enough for the load current, from the copper's current density. All channel currents return through E, which can carry up to 2.5 A, so its trace should be very wide. In the example layout E (pin 8) goes to GND and pin 9 is VCOM."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Test circuits",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "ICEX is measured with the input open (or at a set VI for the ULQ2004A) and VCE applied, COM open. II(off) is the input current that holds IC at 500 μA with VCE = 50 V. II is measured at a set VI with the output open. IR is the clamp diode leakage with VR between COM and the output, input open. VF is measured with IF forced from the output into COM. VCE(sat) and hFE use a current source at the input and one at the output; VI(on) uses an adjustable input voltage with IC forced and VCE held."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Propagation delay and latch-up test",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Delays are measured from the 50 % point of the input edge to the 50 % point of the output edge. The latch-up test drives one input from a pulse generator (PRR = 12.5 kHz, ZO = 50 Ω) with a 40 μs pulse measured at 1.5 V, rise no slower than 5 ns and fall no slower than 10 ns between 10 % and 90 %. VIH is 3 V for the ULQ2003A and 8 V for the ULQ2004A. The output drives 200 Ω in series with 2 mH to VS, with a 1N3064 diode across that load and CL = 15 pF (probe and jig included) to ground; COM is open."
  }
 ],
